Add the successes together. If the total is equal to or higher than the
difficulty, the check succeeds. If it is lower, the check fails. If the final
total is zero or negative, the check is a *critical failure*: the character not
only fails, but something has gone actively wrong. Later chapters define
specific critical failure consequences for attacks, powers, resistance checks,
and other specialized procedures.

Bonuses and penalties usually change the number of dice you roll. A +2d bonus
adds two dice to the pool. A -2d penalty removes two dice from the pool. Bonuses
and penalties stack with each other. If penalties reduce the pool to zero dice,
the check fails automatically.

#note[
  *Example:* Lira has Marksmanship 6. She fires at a distant target for a -2d
  range penalty, so she rolls 4 dice. If she also took aim or received a bonus
  that gave +2d, she would roll 6 dice instead.
]

Some rules add automatic successes or failures. These are added directly to the
result of the roll without changing the number of dice. Whenever a rule
requires you to divide a number and the result is not a whole number, round down
unless the rule specifically says otherwise.

Other dice appear in damage rolls, tables, and random results. When the rules
write a die roll as _XdY_, roll X dice with Y sides and add their faces
together. If the second number is omitted, the rule means six-sided dice.

== Time and Modes of Play

Time is one of Ashen's main sources of pressure. Deathless characters can return
from death, but they cannot always recover lost hours, missed chances, broken
equipment, spent Essence, or allies who died while they were delayed. For that
reason, the game shifts between different modes depending on how closely time
must be tracked.

To make this easier, Ashen utilizes an abstract time structure to divide up
a campaign. At the top level, a campaign can be thought of as being like a 
play -- divided into a sequence of Acts, separated by Intermissions. Each Act
is itself divided into multiple Scenes. 

=== Acts

An Act is a meaningful unit of play---a period during which the characters 
come together to further their shared goals and develop the campaign's plot, if any.
The important thing is that like in a theater, the audience's full attention is on
the stage during an act. An act could be a raid on an enemy fortress, a
harrowing trek across a hostile landscape, or preparing and hosting a diplomatic
gathering with the leaders of other factions.

During an act, the characters' full attention is required. They must rely on the
resources and skills they already have at hand and whatever they manage to find
during the act.

=== Scenes

Acts are divided into _Scenes_. A scene represents a discrete event in the act.
In general, a scene plays out in a single location and deals with a single
issue. The archetypal example of a scene is combat encounter, but other types of
plot events can also constitute a scene: a ride over a collapsing bridge,
haggling with a shady merchant, or exploring a room in a ruined castle.

There are thus almost no hard and fast rules as to what constitutes a scene,
with one exception---a combat encounter is always its own scene. In game terms,
transitioning from one scene to the next has two effects: powers and abilities
that can be used once per scene refresh, and the game can switch from _open
time_ to _structured time_ or vice-versa.

=== Open Time

Most play happens in *open time*. The GM describes the situation, players speak
freely, and actions occur in whatever order makes sense. Open time is used for
conversation, ordinary movement, investigation without immediate danger,
planning, travel details, and other situations where exact turn order is not
important.

Open time can still involve checks and consequences. The difference is that the
game does not need initiative, action points, or a strict sequence of turns to
resolve what happens.

=== Structured Time

When pressure increases and order matters, the game enters *structured time*.
Structured time is used when several characters are acting under danger or time
pressure and the table needs to know who acts first, how much each character can
do, and what happens before the situation changes.

Structured time can be tracked in *rounds* or *stretches*. A round represents a
brief exchange in which each participant takes a turn, usually spending Action
Points (AP) to move, attack, use powers, draw gear, or take other actions.
Combat normally uses rounds.

A stretch represents a longer tense interval, about five minutes in the fiction.
Use stretches when the scene is too pressured for open time but too broad for
round-by-round action: picking a lock while guards approach, searching a chamber
before its owner returns, creeping through a patrolled ruin, or sustaining a
short ritual while the situation changes around the party.

=== Modes of Play

Concurrent with the different forms of time structure, Ashen has three major
modes of play: Encounters, Exploration, and Downtime. Encounters and Exploration are
the contents of scenes, whereas the game enters Downtime mode when an act ends and
the campaign goes into an Intermission.

=== Encounters

An encounter consists, in a word, of dealing with an issue or surmounting an obstacle
of some kind. Encounters may consist, mechanically, of combat, skill checks, or pure role-playing, depending on what kind of encounter the party faces.

==== Combat

Combat is the archetypal form of encounter and uses
the most detailed form of structured time. It focuses on positioning,
initiative, AP, attacks, reactions, damage, defenses, conditions, movement,
summoning, and vehicles. The Combat chapter owns the tactical procedure; this
chapter only establishes that combat is the mode used when violent conflict
needs exact timing and consequences.

Character options often matter in combat even when they are chosen in other
chapters. Attributes, characteristics, advances, powers, weapons, armor,
catalysts, reliquaries, Bulk, Charge, Evasion, Armor, Ward, HP, FP, and AP all
shape what a character can do once structured time begins.

=== Exploration

Exploration covers dangerous movement through the world: travel, Fog exposure,
hazards, disease, rest, supply pressure, soul anchors, and the consequences of
death away from the immediate battlefield. Exploration can be handled in open
time, structured time, or a mix of both. A calm journey might be summarized in
days, while crossing a collapsing bridge or searching a ruin before the Fog
thickens might be resolved in rounds or stretches.

=== Intermissions

An *intermission* is downtime between major acts of play. During an intermission
the characters recover, spend XP, allocate skill  points, buy or repair
equipment, maintain their gear, seek instruction, pursue faction obligations,
and prepare for the next objective. During an intermission, characters may
pursue individual goals -- the next act begins when the entire party is on stage again.
Intermissions are where the campaign takes a breather, but they are still part of the game: the world may change, patrons may make demands, and new opportunities may appear.

== Using This Book

The rest of the rulebook is organized by the kind of decision or procedure you
need.

- Read *Characters* when creating a character, calculating statistics, or
  understanding what the numbers on the sheet mean.
- Read *Races* when choosing what kind of being your Deathless is and what that
  means in the setting.
- Read *Skills* when a non-combat action needs clear difficulty or procedure.
- Read *Advances* when spending XP or planning long-term character growth.
- Read *Magic and Powers* when choosing or resolving spells, prayers, psychic
  powers, exploits, rituals, and command powers.
- Read *Equipment* when buying, maintaining, modifying, or comparing gear.
- Read *Combat* when running tactical fights, maneuvers, conditions, summoning,
  or vehicles.
- Read *Exploration* when travel, Fog, disease, rest, economy, or soul anchors
  matter.
- Read *Setting* when you need the world, factions, gods, geography, and
  history.
- Read *GM Guide* when preparing adventures, rewards, creatures, campaigns, and
  rules adjustments.
